WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.920
Missing threat modeling leaves teams unaware of how checkout flows, refunds,

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.140
loyalty points, stored payment methods, administrative approvals,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.520
or partner integrations can be abused.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.220
Design review should happen before implementation choices become expensive to change.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.160
Unsafe workflows can allow attackers to manipulate business logic even when individual

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.940
endpoints appear to work as coded.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.760
Payment applications need controls for sequence enforcement, rate limits, approval paths,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.620
fraud checks, and transaction integrity.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.400
Weak architectural decisions often involve misplaced trust in internal networks,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.340
client-side state, third-party callbacks, or service-to-service communication.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.360
Payment systems need clear trust boundaries and explicit controls at each boundary.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:52.860
Abuse-case analysis asks what a malicious user, compromised account, insider,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.760
or automated bot would do.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.860
That perspective helps teams design safeguards that ordinary functional requirements may

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.000
miss. Use threat modeling for payment workflows and trust boundaries.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.100
Include security requirements during design and development.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.760
Review architecture for abuse cases before production release.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:18.500
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req. 6.2 - Secure SDLC; Req.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:25.440
6.3 - Security requirements during development; Req. 12.2 - Risk assessments.
